I’m going to give you a complete, one‑file starting point for a simple booking app that follows all the safety habits we talked about.  
I’ll use a **Green** level because it’s just a single‑page calendar‑like list where people can see and book a slot (no logins, no personal data, no payments).  
After you paste the code into your project, you can run it locally and see that it works. I’ll also add comments explaining what each part does and how you can extend it later if you decide to add logins or payments.

> **Remember:** Before you run this, make sure you have `npm` (or `yarn`) and a React project set up. If you’re new to that, tell me your environment (Create‑React‑App, Vite, etc.) and I’ll give a quick guide.

---

## 1️⃣ The complete file (React + Zustand + LocalStorage)

```tsx
// src/App.tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import create from 'zustand';
import './App.css';

/* ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   1.  State store (Zustand) – holds all bookings in memory.
   2.  LocalStorage sync – so data survives page reloads.
   3.  Simple UI – shows a list of slots, lets you "book" a slot.
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*/

interface Slot {
  id: string;          // unique identifier
  date: string;        // ISO string (e.g., "2026-11-25")
  time: string;        // "09:00 AM"
  booked: boolean;     // true when someone books
}

interface BookingState {
  slots: Slot[];
  addSlot: (slot: Slot) => void;
  bookSlot: (id: string) => void;
}

const useStore = create<BookingState>((set) => ({
  slots: [],

  addSlot: (slot) =>
    set((state) => ({
      slots: [...state.slots, slot],
    })),

  bookSlot: (id) =>
    set((state) => ({
      slots: state.slots.map((s) =>
        s.id === id ? { ...s, booked: true } : s
      ),
    })),
}));

/* ── Persist to localStorage (client‑only) ────────────────────────
   This keeps our data safe on the same device, not shared.
 */
const STORAGE_KEY = 'booking-app-slots';

function loadFromLocalStorage(): Slot[] {
  const data = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  if (!data) return [];
  try {
    return JSON.parse(data);
  } catch {
    return [];
  }
}

function saveToLocalStorage(slots: Slot[]) {
  localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(slots));
}

/* ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   2.  The main App component
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*/
function App() {
  const { slots, addSlot, bookSlot } = useStore();

  /* Initialise with a simple pre‑defined set of slots */
  useEffect(() => {
    const stored = loadFromLocalStorage();
    if (stored.length > 0) return; // already loaded

    const initial: Slot[] = [
      {
        id: 'a1',
        date: '2026-12-01',
        time: '09:00 AM',
        booked: false,
      },
      {
        id: 'a2',
        date: '2026-12-01',
        time: '10:00 AM',
        booked: false,
      },
      {
        id: 'b1',
        date: '2026-12-02',
        time: '02:00 PM',
        booked: false,
      },
    ];
    initial.forEach(addSlot);
  }, []); // run once

  /* Sync any change back to localStorage */
  useEffect(() => {
    saveToLocalStorage(slots);
  }, [slots]);

  /* Render a simple table of slots */
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <h1>Simple Booking App</h1>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Date</th>
            <th>Time</th>
            <th>Status</th>
            <th>Action</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {slots.map((s) => (
            <tr key={s.id}>
              <td>{s.date}</td>
              <td>{s.time}</td>
              <td>{s.booked ? 'Booked' : 'Available'}</td>
              <td>
                {!s.booked && (
                  <button onClick={() => bookSlot(s.id)}>Book</button>
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        This is a Green‑level app – no logins, no personal data, no payments.
        All data lives in your browser’s localStorage, so it stays only on the
        device you’re using.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

### What the code does

| Line / Section | Why it’s safe / useful |
|----------------|------------------------|
| `useStore` | Keeps all booking state in memory – no external database needed. |
| `localStorage` sync | Data stays only on the same device; it never leaves the client. |
| `addSlot`, `bookSlot` | Only toggle a `booked` flag; no user credentials involved. |
| `bookSlot` button | Only available if the slot isn’t already booked. |
| No API keys, no external services | Keeps the app simple and self‑contained. |

---

## 2️⃣ How to run it

1. **Create a React app** (if you don’t have one already):

   ```bash
   npx create-react-app my-booking-app
   cd my-booking-app
   ```

2. **Add Zustand** (state library):

   ```bash
   npm install zustand
   ```

3. **Replace** `src/App.tsx` (or `App.jsx` for plain JS) with the code above.

4. **Add a small CSS file** (`src/App.css`) to make the table look nicer (optional):

   ```css
   .App {
     font-family: sans-serif;
     padding: 20px;
   }
   table {
     width: 100%;
     border-collapse: collapse;
     margin-top: 20px;
   }
   th, td {
     border: 1px solid #ddd;
     padding: 8px;
     text-align: left;
   }
   button {
     padding: 5px 10px;
   }
   ```

